WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
Last time, power peaked at a middle load, about a third of the force the muscle can

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.160
hold. Now make it lift that load again and again.

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.000
Its holding force dips a little.

4
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.980
Why can its power drop so much further?

5
00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:23.913
Fatigue is a fall in force or power during repeated activity that recovers with

6
00:00:23.913 --> 00:00:28.233
rest. It usually lowers the ceiling, its holding force.

7
00:00:29.593 --> 00:00:31.393
It often lowers the top speed too.

8
00:00:32.713 --> 00:00:35.273
And in most studies, the speed curve sags more.

9
00:00:36.317 --> 00:00:40.677
In the made-up model muscle, peak power starts near 3.8 watts.

10
00:00:42.017 --> 00:00:45.717
Cut the ceiling by 20 percent, from 100 newtons to 80.

11
00:00:46.977 --> 00:00:49.297
Peak power falls by the same 20 percent.

12
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:53.460
Now let the top speed fall by 20 percent as well.

13
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.640
That makes two cuts of 20 percent each.

14
00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.900
Does peak power now fall by 40 percent?

15
00:01:06.017 --> 00:01:11.577
Not quite. Power is force times speed, so the two cuts multiply.

16
00:01:13.077 --> 00:01:15.097
0.8 times 0.8 is 0.64.

17
00:01:16.637 --> 00:01:20.277
Peak power falls 36 percent, to about 2.4 watts.

18
00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:24.867
Now let the speed curve sag more, as it does in most studies.

19
00:01:26.287 --> 00:01:31.867
In this example, peak power falls about 53 percent in all, to about 1.8 watts.

20
00:01:33.017 --> 00:01:38.777
Here is the rule. Fatigue lowers the ceiling, and often the top speed and the

21
00:01:38.777 --> 00:01:39.777
curve's shape too.

22
00:01:41.057 --> 00:01:44.917
Power depends on all of them at once, so it can fall further than force.

23
00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:47.530
So what pulls these down?

24
00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:53.930
The popular story blames lactic acid, but the evidence points away from the lactate

25
00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:59.730
ion. Leading suspects are inorganic phosphate and failing calcium release.

26
00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:04.650
Protons count too, to a smaller degree near body temperature.

27
00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.410
Their shares are still debated.

28
00:02:08.533 --> 00:02:10.253
Do they act on the motor itself?

29
00:02:11.573 --> 00:02:17.413
In a laser trap, purified myosin heads pulled on one actin filament, with no lactate

30
00:02:17.413 --> 00:02:22.293
present. Acid cut their average peak force by about 20 percent.

31
00:02:23.673 --> 00:02:27.913
Phosphate, added at that low pH, largely abolished the strongest pulls.

32
00:02:29.453 --> 00:02:30.793
Myosin seemed to let go sooner.

33
00:02:31.867 --> 00:02:36.487
In living rats, twitch force tracked phosphate more consistently than protons.

34
00:02:37.627 --> 00:02:40.267
That is an association, not proof of cause.

35
00:02:41.607 --> 00:02:44.987
How much each matters in working human muscle is still open.

36
00:02:46.017 --> 00:02:50.257
Back in the model, the tired muscle still held 80 percent of its force.

37
00:02:51.517 --> 00:02:53.197
Its power fell by more than half.

38
00:02:54.557 --> 00:02:58.217
Power is force times speed, so it pays for every loss at once.
